The seed prompt proposes an initial attack concept given the benign task and adversarial objective.
The compose prompt selects a task-relevant principle subset Ci ⊆Pbase (typically 2–4 principles) and
rewrites the seed to apply them simultaneously. Because selection is stochastic, different runs yield
different compositions. The refine prompt removes overtly adversarial wording and incorporates
any strategies from the feedback library S(r). This is where the two loops connect: discovered
strategies are appended as guidance that the attacker LLM uses at its own judgment, which they guide
generation rather than force specific content. An example refine prompt is shown in Appendix B.
3.4
Failure-Driven Strategy Discovery
After executing the victim CUA on all tasks, SIR collects every attack whose adversarial objective
was not achieved and treats these failures as the primary supervision signal, passing the corresponding
trajectories to an analyzer LLM, which performs two operations:
Diagnose: The analyzer inspects the failed trajectories as a batch and identifies recurring failure
patterns, such as source distrust, target sensitivity, causal implausibility, and detection of imperative
language. Each pattern corresponds to a specific defensive behavior of the victim CUA.
5


